-## Draft decision D102
-
-1. Intervention (interchange intervention, Geiger et al. 2021): for an ordered pair (recipient r, donor d) of the two members of a pair, during the recipient's full generation (all 12 passes), replace at layer k* = 2 (the D101(3) layer) the recipient's prefix residual stream at every cell position that is empty in both members, as follows:
-   - probe subspace: x_r ← x_r + Q Qᵀ (x_d − x_r), with Q (512 × 9) an orthonormal basis of the nine digit rows of the frozen pilot P2 shared head restricted to the state coordinates (the cell one-hot part is dropped);
-   - random subspace control: the same with Q a random orthonormal 512 × 9 basis (5 fixed seeds);
-   - full-vector swap (ceiling): x_r ← x_d at those positions;
-   - sham (identity check): x_r ← x_r + Q Qᵀ (x_r − x_r); generated codes must equal the clean run bit for bit.
-   Clue positions and positions empty in only one member are never patched, so the recipient keeps its own clue tokens. Both orders (a→b, b→a) are run for every PROBE-VAL pair (500 recipient runs per condition).
-2. Representational outcome (does the downstream representation follow the donor?): after patching at layer 2, the recipient's prefix states at layers 4 and 8 are read with the frozen pilot shared heads of those layers; donor-follow rate = share of discordant bits (cell empty in both, digit candidate in exactly one member) on which the patched recipient's probability moves towards the donor's label relative to the clean recipient run.
-3. Behavioural outcomes: (a) code change: share of recipient runs whose generated codes at levels 7–12 differ from the clean run, and per-level code change counts; (b) decoded-grid change: cell Hamming distance between the patched and clean decoded grids; (c) rescue: on the 51 pairs with exactly one solved member, solve rate of the unsolved member when the solved member is the donor, and the converse harm rate.
-4. Primary pilot contrast: per recipient, probe-subspace effect minus the mean effect of the five random subspaces of equal rank, for the donor-follow rate and for the code-change indicator; paired over recipients, pair-level bootstrap (resampling pairs, both orders together). The full swap bounds what any prefix-cell intervention at layer 2 can change; the sham must show zero change.
-5. Readout of the result for the protocol: probe-subspace effect clearly above the random subspaces on the behavioural outcomes → the generator uses the candidate directions of its puzzle encoding; probe-subspace effect only on the representational outcome → the directions propagate inside the encoder but do not drive code choice; no effect beyond random → the readable candidate information is not used through this subspace (not that it is unused in general). Thresholds are fixed in the protocol, not here.
-6. Cost (measured basis: generation 1.7 s per puzzle on CPU, `experiments/e5/cost.json`): 8 conditions (sham, probe, 5 random, full) × 500 recipient runs ≈ 1.9 h.
-
-## Open questions for the review
-
-- Is patching at all both-empty cell positions in every pass the right scope, or should the intervention be restricted to the passes after level 6 (keeping Z<=6 fixed) to separate the effect on Z<=6 from the effect on later levels?
-- Is a nine-dimensional probe subspace from one linear head, applied position-wise, a fair "candidate subspace", and is a random orthonormal subspace of equal rank the right control (norm of the projected difference, alignment with high-variance directions)?
-- Is the donor-follow rate well defined when the clean recipient prediction is already saturated (P2 probabilities near 0 or 1)?
-- Does the shared solution make behavioural outcomes uninformative (both members should produce the same grid), and is the rescue analysis on 51 pairs too small to be useful?
-- Are there identity checks missing (for example, that patching with the recipient's own activations at all positions reproduces the clean codes, and that the hook does not alter the query positions)?
+Architecture facts the design relies on (`third_party/ncm/models/next_concept.py`, `src/ncm_sudoku/intervene.py`): generation runs 12 passes, one per level; each pass processes the 91-token puzzle prefix plus that level's query positions; prefix positions attend only to the prefix (`gen_mask[:, :, :P, P:] = -inf`, `m0` likewise), query positions attend to the prefix and to each other; a patch at a prefix position can reach the queries only through attention in later blocks of the same pass, and across levels through the codes; same-position patches leave RoPE untouched; decoding is greedy (temperature 0) and bit-deterministic for a fixed batch shape (D20). Layer convention (`e5extract.py`): layer 0 = input of `blocks[0]`, layer k = output of `blocks[k−1]`; layer 2 is the output of `blocks[1]`. Pair facts on PROBE-VAL: 88 of 250 pairs have identical level 1–6 codes; the clean members' level 7–12 codes differ in 249 of 250 pairs (mean 31.8 of 252 codes), so a directed code target exists; both members are solved in 194 pairs and exactly one in 51. The layer-2 donor−recipient differences on both-empty cells are strongly anisotropic: on 8,103 cells the top 9 singular directions carry 49.5% of the squared norm, against 1.8% expected for an isotropic 9-dimensional projection.
+
+## Draft decision D102 (revised after R1)
+
+1. Probe heads and subspace. The P2, P4 and P8 shared own-position heads are refitted deterministically at their selected pilot configurations (same data, seeds, budget), checked to reproduce the saved pilot predictions (`runs/e5/pilot/P{2,4,8}__shared__none.npy`), and saved (weights, biases). Q (512 × 9) is a float64 orthonormal basis (QR/SVD, rank check sigma_9 > 0) of the seed-averaged state rows of the P2 head (the cell one-hot part drops out of a same-cell difference); bases of the individual seeds are a sensitivity check.
+2. Intervention (interchange intervention, Geiger et al. 2021). For an ordered pair (recipient r, donor d) of the two members of a pair, at the output of `blocks[1]` (layer 2), at every prefix cell position empty in both members, the recipient's state is replaced by x_r + ((x_d − x_r) Q) Qᵀ, computed on a cloned output; clue positions and positions empty in only one member are never patched (the probes were trained on empty cells only; patching one-sided positions would inject clue content). Donor activations are collected online in fp32 from the same pass and the same batch layout as the recipient run (not from the saved fp16 single-pass arrays). Both orders are run for every PROBE-VAL pair and averaged per pair.
+3. Scope. Primary: patch only the passes that generate levels 7–12, with the recipient's clean level 1–6 codes fixed (`intervene.continue_generation(..., n_given_levels=6)`; primary outcome read at the level-7 pass with `intervene.level_logits(..., level=7)`), so Z<=6 is held fixed and only direct reading of the prefix after level 6 is tested. Secondary: patch all 12 passes, reporting level 1–6 code changes and the resulting shift of Z<=6 separately from level 7–12 changes.
+4. Conditions. (a) Probe subspace Q. (b) Norm-matched random subspaces: random orthonormal 512 × 9 bases (float64 QR of i.i.d. Gaussian matrices, positive R diagonal, fixed seeds), with the injected step rescaled per cell to the norm of the probe-subspace step; 10–20 draws in the one-pass level-7 evaluation, fewer in full generation; spread across draws reported. (c) In-manifold control: a 9-dimensional subspace orthogonal to Q drawn from the leading principal directions of PROBE-TRAIN both-empty donor−recipient differences projected off Q, norm-matched per cell; it leaves the P2 candidate logits unchanged by construction. (d) Shuffled-digit control in the same subspace: the same norm-matched step aimed at a donor target whose digit labels are permuted, to separate digit-specific from generic effects. (e) Both-empty full-swap reference: x_r ← x_d at the both-empty positions, and a full donor-prefix reference at fixed Z<=6 (`level_logits` with the donor's prefix and the recipient's codes); these are references, not ceilings (non-linear blocks and argmax do not make a subspace patch weaker than a full swap). (f) Identity checks: no hook; self-donor (d := r through the whole donor-collection and indexing path, at both-empty positions and at all 91 prefix positions) must reproduce the clean codes and logits bit for bit; query isolation: at the hooked block, the query rows and the unpatched prefix rows of the output equal the clean run bit for bit. The injected step norm ||((x_d − x_r) Q) Qᵀ|| is logged per cell and run for every condition.
+5. Outcomes. Primary (directed, pre-argmax, at the level-7 pass with fixed Z<=6): movement of the recipient's 16-way code distributions over the 18 level-7 queries towards the full-swap reference, TV(P_clean, P_full) − TV(P_cond, P_full), averaged over queries; the undirected TV(P_cond, P_clean) is reported as a perturbation diagnostic. Representational (inside the encoder): with the refitted P4 and P8 heads in float64 logit space using the seed-averaged logit z, on discordant bits (cell empty in both, digit candidate in exactly one member): the signed shift (2 y_d − 1)(z_cond − z_clean), the decision flip rate towards y_d, and the normalized shift (z_cond − z_clean)/(z_donor − z_clean) on bits the clean probe orders correctly; fixed numerical tolerance; a non-finite value stops the run. Behavioural (full generation, descriptive): code changes split into donor-following switches (c_cond = c_full ≠ c_clean) and off-target switches (c_cond ∉ {c_clean, c_full}); solve rate, clue preservation and format errors as damage diagnostics over all runs; rescue and harm on the 51 one-solved pairs as counts with intervals, not as decision criteria.
+6. Primary pilot contrast: per pair (both orders averaged), the probe-subspace effect minus the mean effect of the norm-matched random subspaces, and minus the in-manifold control, for the primary outcome and the representational signed shift; pair-level bootstrap, stated as conditional on the drawn random bases; rare-event and zero-count rules as in D101.
+7. Readout for the protocol (thresholds fixed later, before TEST): a probe-subspace effect above the norm-matched and in-manifold controls on the primary outcome, and above the shuffled-digit control → the generator's level-7 code distribution is sensitive to the candidate directions of its puzzle encoding in a digit-specific way; an effect only on the representational outcome → the directions propagate inside the encoder but do not move code choice; no effect beyond controls → no detectable dependence on this subspace under this intervention (not that the candidate information is unused in general). Without the shuffled-digit contrast, only sensitivity to this intervention is claimed.
+8. Cost plan: refitting and saving three heads (minutes at the selected configurations); the primary one-pass level-7 evaluation (one recipient forward per condition and draw, cheap); full generation at about 1.7 s per puzzle per condition (`experiments/e5/cost.json`) plus one clean/donor collection run; measured first on a small batch with the real batch size before the full pilot.
